# Hot vertex pages kept current by hash (27 Nov): the 0111 diagnostic showed pages a vertex request re-uploads frame
# after frame cause ~90-98% of the re-protects at explosions (each an OS protection call under the SDK's global lock,
# plus a write fault on the game thread), while the draws reading them request only ~2 KB each. A page a vertex
# request had to upload 3 frames running becomes hot: after its next CPU write it is never protected again; each
# vertex request that needs it hashes it (XXH3 of 4 KB) and copies it in place only when the hash differs from what
# the GPU copy holds (hashed before that copy), queueing the change for the texture and index caches since no fault
# reports it; the index table doesn't reuse conversions over hot pages. A hot page unchanged for 120 frames, or
# requested by anything but a vertex fetch, goes back to protection. On by default (tested 27 Nov: write faults ~10 a
# frame instead of ~120-220, ~12% less render-thread time per draw); BOTS_GFX_HOT_PAGES=0 turns it off. With
# BOTS_DRAW_PROFILE=1 a line per stats window, "hot vertex pages per frame".
